From: HiBreak Pro Color <hibreak@localhost>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 18:16:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] HiBreak Pro Color: Android 16 vendor/a9_services essentials

Ported from the validated 22.2 baseline; keep the existing kernel/vendor.
Require WRITE_SECURE_SETTINGS from senders of the exported E-Ink control broadcasts.
Deliver E-Ink command batches in order on a worker thread instead of the main thread.
Hyper raises min_refresh_rate to persist.vendor.xrz.fps (default 120) instead of a fixed 90.
The Video scene follows the factory video preset: Fast with auto-clean and anti-flicker (was Hyper).
Run a9_eink_server as system (init hands it the frontlight nodes); persist.debug.hibreak.einkd_root=1 restores the root instance.
Scene taps coalesce: RefreshModeManager and DisplaySettingsManager post one profile and one display batch per preference transaction instead of one per key. Frontlight level changes no longer write Settings.System screen_brightness_cold/warm (only the one-time migration reads them) or move the scene alarm (scene and schedule keys still do); no per-step frontlight or daemon property logs. Button mapping keys and defaults come from one table (ButtonActionManager), so Settings shows Nothing for the screen-off single press as the service does. BootReceiver is not exported.
Unused code removed: daemon commands no client sends (display, b/s/p, mode<N>, br_co/br_wm; frontlight writes are capped at 220 like the client), the allow_custom_broadcast receiver and its setting, unused Commands constants, WaveformMode.command and RefreshModeManager helpers (changeDither, applyWaveform, waveformFor, currentDither), ColorProgressPreference, Utils.kt, dead SDK checks and the close_status_bar branch, about 40 unused drawables, two layouts, colors.xml, unused arrays and strings (en, zh), the backup rules, ota.json and the unwired sepolicy directory; README keeps the licensing. DisplayScene uses the DisplayProfilePreferences keys. zh: Toggle Night Light is 切换夜间光效 (it toggles Night Light).
Frontlight Day/Night: the selected scene is inverted (black fill, white text) instead of a thicker stroke, and both buttons use the same whole-pixel edge (round(density), 2px at 300dpi); the unselected button's 1px (dp(1) truncated) edge could disappear. The waveform/dither segmented choices use the same whole-pixel edge.
Frontlight Day/Night and the panel segmented choices no longer align text baselines: the selected label's check mark falls back to Noto Sans Symbols (taller ascent than Roboto), so baseline alignment moved the unselected button 2px down and the group clipped its bottom edge. The Day/Night buttons also match the group height.
---

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 28c4258..6fe082a 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -1,29 +1,13 @@
-Automatically patch system.img for use with Hibreak Pro, adding eink features support
-For advanced users, check xda for pre-patched images.
+# HiBreak Pro Color E-Ink services
 
-To run the script on linux and patch your own treble based system.img you simply have to:
+The E-Ink settings app and accessibility service (`A9AccessibilityService`, package
+`net.dianqk.hibreak.eink`) and the E-Ink control daemon (`a9_eink_server`) built into the
+HiBreak Pro Color GSI. Derived from the a9_accessibility_service project by damianmqr
+(https://github.com/damianmqr/a9_accessibility_service), as packaged by
+vbbot/HibreakProPatchingService.
 
-- Download a treble-droid based system.img you want to use
-- Download and extract a9_system_patcher.zip from https://github.com/damianmqr/a9_accessibility_service/releases/latest
-- Go in terminal to the folder containing unzipped files
-- Run `sudo bash patch_system_img.sh /path/to/system.img`
-- system_patched.img will be saved in the same directory, ready to be flashed
-
-Flashing the resulting file is done the same as any other system.img
-**(make sure your bootloader is unlocked! and you have disabled vbmeta verification by reflashing with `fastboot flash vbmeta --disable-verity --disable-verification vbmeta.img`)**
-
-- reboot to fastbootd using `adb reboot fastboot` (or any other method)
-- run `fastboot flash system system_patched.img`
-- after that's done, run `fastboot -w`
-- run `fastboot reboot` and give it a few minutes
-
-Default E-ink features Usage:
-
-**Single Press E-Ink Button** - Refresh Screen
-
-**Double Press E-Ink Button** - Open E-Ink Menu with settings for Per-App refresh modes.
-
-These mappings can be easily changed in the E-Ink Settings app
+Default E-Ink button actions: a single press refreshes the screen, a double press opens the
+E-Ink menu with per-app display settings. The mappings can be changed in the E-Ink settings app.
 
 ## Licensing
 
